// Contract content (GAMEPLAY.md §6/§9) — data, never hardcoded in sim_core.
// This is the `contracts.ron` CLAUDE.md's Project Structure section and
// GAMEPLAY.md §9 have both been anticipating ("Contracts need their own data
// file ... following the same schema_version convention as the other data
// files"); it replaces the single `Contract::hardcoded()` milestone 5's
// Economy MVP shipped with, and — more importantly — it is what makes
// SEVERAL contracts able to run at once.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader (`sim_core::sim::contracts::ContractRegistry`) rejects a
// mismatched version rather than silently misreading a changed shape, the
// same way `recipes.ron`'s and `machines.ron`'s loaders do.
//
// SCHEMA 2 (2026-09-10, the contract-board epic): this file is a BOARD OF
// OFFERS, not the list of what is running. A new game starts with only the
// contracts marked `starter: true` active; every other contract here is an
// offer the player SIGNS from the contracts board (the C key) for its
// `price`, out of the one money counter. A contract that misses `strikes`
// windows IN A ROW is LOST — it leaves the active list and goes back on the
// board, and the player has to pay its price again to get it back. One
// signature per contract: a contract is either on the board or active,
// never both and never twice. v1 files (no `price`/`strikes`, all contracts
// active from tick 0) are rejected, not migrated.
//
// WHAT A CONTRACT IS (GAMEPLAY.md §6). A standing agreement to supply a
// `mix` of items every `cadence_ticks` ticks, for `payout` money each time
// the mix is delivered in full. It is deliberately NOT a spot market: the
// mix is all-or-nothing, so half a delivery earns nothing, and that is the
// whole reason the game rewards a factory that can hold several different
// lines running at once rather than one maximally-profitable one.
//
// FILE ORDER IS PRIORITY. Every active contract draws from the same stock
// — what stands on every shipping dock, summed (see `sim_core/src/sim/economy.rs`'s
// module doc for the full allocation rule and why it is the way it is). When
// two contracts fall due on the same tick and there isn't enough stock for
// both, the one listed FIRST here is filled first. That is a deterministic
// tiebreak (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9), not a coincidence of iteration order —
// reordering this list genuinely changes who gets paid under scarcity. A
// contract signed later still takes its FILE position in the active list,
// so signing order never changes priority.
//
// FIELDS, per contract:
//   id            stable content id (save files and tests key on it)
//   name          what the player reads
//   mix           [(item, quantity)] required per delivery, all-or-nothing
//   cadence_ticks how often the mix is checked
//   payout        money per satisfied window
//   price         money to sign it (and to sign it AGAIN after losing it);
//                 at least 1, so a lost contract is never free to recover
//   strikes       consecutive missed windows that lose the contract; at
//                 least 1 (1 = a single miss loses it). A paid window resets
//                 the count to zero.
//   starter       (optional, default false) active in a new game without
//                 being signed. At least one contract must be a starter.
//
// THE BOARD ASKS FOR TWO SAUCES (2026-09-18, the BBQ epic). Until BBQ landed,
// every contract here asked for `boxed_ketchup` — not as a design statement
// but because the chain produced exactly one sellable product, so a contract
// naming anything else would have been permanently unsatisfiable content
// rather than interesting content. GAMEPLAY.md §6's central claim — that
// contracts demand a *mix*, so a diversified factory is the correct strategy
// — was therefore fully implemented, fully tested, and never once exercised
// in play.
//
// `data/recipes.ron` now has a second branch, so the BBQ tier at the bottom
// of this file is that claim finally put in front of the player: two of its
// four orders name both sauces, and because a mix is all-or-nothing, a
// factory that makes plenty of ketchup and no BBQ earns nothing from them.
// What the board creates is still the same ladder of commitment (bigger
// orders, stiffer terms, better rates) whose bottom rungs one line can carry
// and whose top rungs keep asking for one more line — it now has a second
// rail.
//
// THREE SAUCES SINCE THE MUSTARD TIER (2026-09-26). The tier at the very
// bottom of this file is the same idea one step on: BBQ was a branch off the
// ketchup line, so a BBQ order is a claim on capacity the player already
// owns, while mustard is a whole second line from raw, so a mustard order is
// a claim on floor, belt and crew that do not exist yet. Its mixed order
// (`deli_chain`) therefore asks for something `grill_pub` cannot — two lines
// running at once rather than one line split two ways.
//
// BALANCE NOTE (rewritten 2026-09-11, when the board grew from four
// contracts to nine — the growth tier was written against the three-contract
// board and merged with the board-of-offers' fourth on 2026-09-12). The
// shipped chain's bottleneck is the 240-tick cooking vat, so ONE line's
// steady-state throughput is roughly one `boxed_ketchup` per ~245 ticks,
// i.e. about 4.0 per 1000 ticks. The board is sized in that unit — "lines'
// worth of demand":
//
//   starter tier   (corner_shop + diner_supply + market_stall)  ~3.9 / 1000
//   growth tier    (the six orders below them)                 ~19.2 / 1000
//   BBQ tier       (the four below those, in VAT output)        ~7.0 / 1000
//   mustard tier   (the four at the bottom)                     ~5.1 / 1000
//   whole board                                                ~35.1 / 1000
//
// The BBQ tier is quoted in vat output rather than in boxes because that is
// the only unit the two sauces share: it asks for 2.5 boxed_ketchup and ~4.5
// boxed_bbq per 1000 ticks, and every one of those BBQ boxes eats a ketchup
// the same vats had to make. Counting it any other way understates what a
// mixed order costs to serve, which is the whole point of it.
//
// The mustard tier's ~5.1 is ~4.1 `boxed_mustard` plus 1.0 `boxed_ketchup`
// per 1000, and those mustard boxes are NOT interchangeable with the numbers
// above them: a mustard line's bottleneck is a 360-tick vat rather than a
// 240-tick one, so one mustard line is ~2.7 boxes per 1000 where a ketchup
// line is ~4.0. In lines' worth — the only unit that compares the two
// honestly — the tier is about **one and a half mustard lines plus a quarter
// of a ketchup line**, which takes the whole board from about seven and a
// quarter lines' worth of demand to about **nine**.
//
// The starter tier is deliberately just inside ONE line's capacity — but
// only `corner_shop` is `starter: true` (auto-active from tick 0, per the
// board-of-offers rule above); `diner_supply` and `market_stall` are the
// starter tier's other two rungs, cheap enough (150, 60) to sign within a
// window or two of `corner_shop`'s own payouts, so a new player is meant to
// hold all three within their first several minutes, not from the first
// tick. A first factory kept fed (hoppers and tank on auto-fill) can serve
// all three once signed, and one left to run dry starts missing windows.
// Everything past it is what the second, third, fourth and fifth line are
// FOR. Before the
// growth tier existed, the board absorbed one line's output and a little
// more, so the money a first line earned bought a second line that had
// almost nothing to sell to — the game stalled at exactly the point where a
// factory-builder should be taking off. Now the whole board takes nearly
// six lines, so there is always an order that the next line can go after,
// and a fully built-out board is a real target rather than a ceiling
// reached in the first ten minutes.
//
// Rates per box RISE WITH COMMITMENT, WITHIN A SAUCE: on the ketchup orders
// the small, frequent one pays worst per box (24) and the big, slow pallet
// order pays best (40); on the BBQ orders the same shape runs 60 to 65. The
// qualifier is new with the BBQ tier and it is not a loophole — BBQ pays
// better per box at EVERY size because it is a costlier box to make (23 in
// raws against 11, `data/ingredients.ron`), so comparing a BBQ order's rate
// against a ketchup order's says nothing about commitment. Big orders are
// also the ones that are impossible to serve from a
// single line — a 20-box pallet on a 5000-tick cadence needs a whole line
// devoted to it — so a better per-box rate is the reward for building the
// capacity to reach it, and a player who cannot yet fill it loses that
// window's payout and, after `strikes` misses in a row, the contract.
//
// MUSTARD RUNS 44 TO 52 PER BOX, between the two, for the same reason: a box
// of mustard costs 17 in raws (`data/ingredients.ron`) against ketchup's 11
// and BBQ's 23, so the third-of-revenue band puts its rungs between the
// other two sauces' rungs. Each payout below shows the arithmetic it came
// from.
//
// **WHY A MUSTARD LINE IS WORTH BUILDING AT ALL, given those rates.** Per
// tick of the vat that limits it, a mustard order pays 44/360 to 52/360 =
// 0.12 to 0.14, and a ketchup order pays 24/240 to 40/240 = 0.10 to 0.17. So
// a mustard line beats serving the starter tier with more ketchup and loses
// to `export_pallet` — which is exactly the decision the tier is for. The
// reason to build one is not a better rate, it is that the ketchup rungs are
// FINITE: once the good ones are served, another ketchup line has nothing
// left to sell to (see the unsold-stock note at the end of this header),
// while the mustard tier is four unserved orders. Capacity is worth what
// there is demand for.
//
// PRIORITY ORDER IS SMALLEST FIRST, on purpose. When several orders fall
// due on the same tick (every 60 000 ticks every cadence on the board lines
// up), the starter orders are filled before the growth tier gets a look
// in — so a first factory's income is never eaten by a pallet order the
// player has not built for, and a big order that CAN be filled still is,
// out of whatever the small ones left. Within the growth tier the order
// again runs small to large.
//
// Signing fees are sized against the reference line's 959 bill and the
// 3000 starting balance: the mid-tier orders cost a window or two of their
// own payout, the top rungs are a real capital decision. All placeholders
// per GAMEPLAY.md §10; change them here and in the prose above together.
//
// A box that no contract has ordered is still ingredients turned into
// unsold stock (`data/ingredients.ron`, ~11 per box of ketchup, ~17 per box
// of mustard and ~23 per box of BBQ), so a line built beyond the board's
// demand is still a loss — and converting ketchup nobody ordered into BBQ
// nobody ordered doubles the loss rather than hedging it. A second line
// makes that easier to do by accident, not harder: mustard shares `vinegar`
// with ketchup, so an overbuilt mustard line spends the ketchup line's
// money as well as its own. The board grew three times; the rule did not.
(
    schema_version: 2,
    contracts: [
        // --- The starter tier ---
        // Exactly milestone 5's hardcoded contract (2 boxes every 1000
        // ticks for 50), kept numerically identical so that the first thing
        // a new player — and the full-chain integration test — sees is
        // unchanged by this file's arrival. Three strikes: a new player's
        // first line WILL miss its first window or two while it is being
        // built, and losing the only contract before the first box ships
        // would be a dead start, not a lesson. Cheap to get back.
        Contract(
            id: "corner_shop",
            name: "Corner Shop Standing Order",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 1000,
            payout: 50,
            price: 40,
            strikes: 3,
            starter: true,
        ),
        // The reason to keep the line running rather than babysitting one
        // window: a bigger box count on a much slower cadence, at a better
        // per-unit rate (30/box). Two strikes.
        Contract(
            id: "diner_supply",
            name: "Roadside Diner Group",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 3)],
            cadence_ticks: 2500,
            payout: 90,
            price: 150,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The small, awkward one: a single box on a cadence that lines up
        // with neither of the others, at the worst per-unit rate (24/box).
        // Its job is to be the order that gets squeezed out when the line
        // stalls — which is exactly the pressure a single contract could
        // never create. ONE strike: a stall holder who misses once is gone.
        Contract(
            id: "market_stall",
            name: "Saturday Market Stall",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 1)],
            cadence_ticks: 1500,
            payout: 24,
            price: 60,
            strikes: 1,
        ),

        // --- The growth tier ---
        // Everything from here down is more than one line can serve
        // alongside the starter tier, by design: it is the demand a second,
        // third, fourth and fifth line are built to meet. Small to large,
        // per the priority rule in the header.

        // A steady institutional order: four boxes on the market stall's
        // cadence, at a rate a shade above the diner's (32/box). The first
        // order a second line makes reachable — a line and a half serves
        // the starter tier and this. Two strikes.
        Contract(
            id: "school_canteens",
            name: "District School Canteens",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 4)],
            cadence_ticks: 1500,
            payout: 128,
            price: 200,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // Six boxes a window at 35/box, on a cadence long enough to bank
        // them — a demand one line cannot carry alongside the starter tier.
        // Two strikes, and an expensive signature to lose.
        Contract(
            id: "regional_grocer",
            name: "Regional Grocer Chain",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 6)],
            cadence_ticks: 4000,
            payout: 210,
            price: 400,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The same six boxes on half the cadence (36/box) — three quarters
        // of a line on its own.
        Contract(
            id: "wholesale_grocer",
            name: "Wholesale Grocer Depot",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 6)],
            cadence_ticks: 2000,
            payout: 216,
            price: 350,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // A whole line's worth on a 2000-tick cadence (36/box): the order
        // that justifies a line of its own.
        Contract(
            id: "burger_franchise",
            name: "Burger Franchise Commissary",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 8)],
            cadence_ticks: 2000,
            payout: 288,
            price: 500,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // Also a whole line's worth, but in bigger, slower batches (38/box):
        // twelve boxes must be standing on the dock at once, so the shared
        // pool has to be allowed to build up rather than skimmed by the
        // starter orders — which is what listing it AFTER them costs the
        // player, and what the higher rate pays for.
        Contract(
            id: "stadium_concessions",
            name: "Stadium Concessions",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 12)],
            cadence_ticks: 3000,
            payout: 456,
            price: 750,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The pallet order: twenty boxes every 5000 ticks at the best rate
        // any KETCHUP order pays (40/box). A whole line's throughput, held
        // back for 5000 ticks — the top of the ketchup ladder, and what the
        // BBQ tier below is measured against.
        Contract(
            id: "export_pallet",
            name: "Export Pallet Order",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 20)],
            cadence_ticks: 5000,
            payout: 800,
            price: 1200,
            strikes: 2,
        ),

        // --- The BBQ tier ---
        // The orders that finally make `mix` mean what GAMEPLAY.md §6 always
        // said it meant. Everything above asks for one sauce; two of the
        // four below ask for two, and an all-or-nothing mix of two sauces is
        // a different demand from the sum of its parts — the factory has to
        // hold BOTH lines running in the same window or the whole delivery
        // earns nothing.
        //
        // LISTED LAST, so the priority rule above is undisturbed: a player
        // who has not built a BBQ branch never sees a BBQ order skim the
        // docks ahead of the ketchup orders they built for. Within the
        // tier the order again runs small to large.
        //
        // NOTE THE EXISTING CONTRACTS ARE UNTOUCHED. Amending a live order
        // to demand BBQ would silently break a factory that was serving it
        // perfectly well, and the player would lose a contract to a patch
        // rather than to a mistake. New demand arrives as new offers.
        //
        // BBQ PAYS ~60 PER BOX where ketchup pays 24-40, and both sit at
        // roughly the same third-of-revenue ingredient ratio: a box of BBQ
        // costs 23 in raws against a box of ketchup's 11
        // (`data/ingredients.ron`), 11 of that 23 being the ketchup it eats.
        // So the per-box rate rule from the note above ("bigger orders pay
        // better") now holds WITHIN each sauce rather than across the whole
        // board — a 2-box BBQ order out-earning a 3-box ketchup order per
        // box is BBQ being worth more, not a small order being overpaid.
        //
        // WHAT THE TIER ASKS FOR, in the balance note's unit. The four add
        // 2.5 boxed_ketchup and ~4.5 boxed_bbq per 1000 ticks. Every box of
        // BBQ eats a box's worth of ketchup, so in VAT output — the thing a
        // line actually produces — that is ~7.0 more per 1000 on top of the
        // ketchup board's ~23.0, taking the whole board from about six
        // lines' worth of demand to about seven and a quarter.

        // The tutorial order for the second sauce: two boxes on a slow
        // cadence, cheap to sign, and THREE strikes where the rest of the
        // tier gets two. That is deliberate and it is the same call
        // `corner_shop` got — this is the first order for a line the player
        // has never built, and a branch will miss a window or two while it
        // is being plumbed. Losing the order that taught you the sauce would
        // be a dead end, not a lesson.
        Contract(
            id: "smokehouse_trial",
            name: "Smokehouse Trial",
            mix: [("boxed_bbq", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 2500,
            payout: 120,
            price: 200,
            strikes: 3,
        ),
        // **THE FIRST MIXED ORDER, AND THE REASON THE COOK IS 600 TICKS.**
        // Three ketchup and two BBQ is not a round number picked for
        // variety: it is exactly what one ketchup line with one BBQ branch
        // bolted onto it naturally produces. The vat makes one ketchup per
        // 240 ticks; the BBQ branch draws one of them per 600; the
        // remainder bottles out as ketchup at one per 400 — so the factory
        // settles at 3 ketchup : 2 BBQ all by itself, and the obvious build
        // serves this order without the player tuning a thing. That is the
        // teaching: a mixed order is a RATIO, and the ratio is a property of
        // the factory you already built.
        //
        // `data/recipes.ron`'s `bbq` entry states the same arithmetic from
        // the other end. The cook time and these mixes move together or not
        // at all.
        Contract(
            id: "grill_pub",
            name: "Grill Pub Chain",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 3), ("boxed_bbq", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 3000,
            payout: 210,
            price: 350,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The same 3:2 shape at double the size on a slower cadence — two
        // lines-plus-branches' worth. Proof that the ratio scales: a player
        // who understood `grill_pub` knows exactly what this one needs, and
        // needs only to build more of it.
        Contract(
            id: "roadhouse_group",
            name: "Roadhouse Group",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 6), ("boxed_bbq", 4)],
            cadence_ticks: 4000,
            payout: 440,
            price: 700,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The BBQ pallet, and the best rate on the board (65/box): ten boxes
        // of the slow sauce every 5000 ticks, which at a 600-tick cook is
        // more than one branch can make — it takes the ketchup output of
        // several lines converted wholesale. `export_pallet`'s counterpart
        // on the BBQ side, and the top of the board.
        Contract(
            id: "bbq_festival",
            name: "Barbecue Festival",
            mix: [("boxed_bbq", 10)],
            cadence_ticks: 5000,
            payout: 650,
            price: 1000,
            strikes: 2,
        ),

        // --- The mustard tier ---
        // The third sauce's orders, and the first demand on the board that
        // cannot be served by re-plumbing the factory the player already has.
        // A BBQ order asks them to split one line's output; these ask them to
        // own a second line, five machines and two hoppers from nothing
        // (`data/shop.ron`), which is why the whole tier waits behind
        // `data/unlocks.ron`'s `mustard_sauce` gate rather than sitting on the
        // board from tick 0.
        //
        // LISTED LAST, so the priority rule in the header is again
        // undisturbed: a player with no mustard line never sees a mustard
        // order skim the shared pool ahead of the orders they did build for,
        // and within the tier the order runs small to large.
        //
        // THE EXISTING ORDERS ARE UNTOUCHED, for the reason the BBQ tier
        // records: new demand arrives as new offers, never as an amendment to
        // a live order a player was serving perfectly well.
        //
        // EVERY PAYOUT BELOW IS DERIVED, not chosen. A box of mustard costs
        // 17 in raws — 4 seed at 2, 1 turmeric at 5, 2 vinegar at 2
        // (`data/ingredients.ron`) — and the header's margin rule puts
        // ingredients at roughly a third of revenue, which is the 44-52 band.
        // Each comment shows its own arithmetic, and the rate rises with
        // commitment inside the sauce: 44, then 46, then 52.

        // The tutorial order for the third sauce, and `smokehouse_trial`'s
        // counterpart: two boxes on a slow cadence, cheap to sign, and THREE
        // strikes — as `hot_dog_stand` below has, where the two larger orders
        // of the tier get two. Same call as
        // `corner_shop` and the BBQ trial — this is the first order for a
        // line the player has never built, and a line built from nothing will
        // miss a window or two while the belt is still going down.
        //
        // PAYOUT: 2 boxes x 44 = 88. 44 is the bottom of the mustard band
        // (17 in raws is 39% of it), and it is the tier's worst rate per box,
        // which is where a tutorial order belongs — `corner_shop` pays 25 on
        // a board whose best ketchup rung is 40.
        Contract(
            id: "deli_counter",
            name: "Deli Counter",
            mix: [("boxed_mustard", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 2500,
            payout: 88,
            price: 200,
            strikes: 3,
        ),
        // One more box on a slightly slower cadence for a slightly better
        // rate: the rung that pays for keeping the new line fed rather than
        // watching one window. Three strikes as well, because a player is
        // likely to sign both of these before the line is finished.
        //
        // PAYOUT: 3 boxes x 46 = 138. One step up the band from
        // `deli_counter`'s 44, for one more box per window.
        Contract(
            id: "hot_dog_stand",
            name: "Hot Dog Stand",
            mix: [("boxed_mustard", 3)],
            cadence_ticks: 3000,
            payout: 138,
            price: 300,
            strikes: 3,
        ),
        // **THE SECOND MIXED ORDER, AND THE REASON THE MUSTARD COOK IS 360
        // TICKS.** Three ketchup and two mustard is what a factory that has
        // finished one line of each produces on its own: the ketchup vat
        // makes one box per 240 ticks, the mustard vat one per 360, and
        // 240:360 is 3:2. So the obvious two-line build serves this order
        // without the player tuning anything — the same teaching `grill_pub`
        // carries, one rung up, except that here the two rates come from two
        // separate factories rather than one factory divided.
        //
        // `data/recipes.ron`'s `mustard` entry states the same arithmetic
        // from the other end. That cook time and this mix move together or
        // not at all.
        //
        // PAYOUT: 3 ketchup x 36 + 2 mustard x 48 = 108 + 96 = 204. The 36 is
        // the mid-tier ketchup rate (`wholesale_grocer`, and what `grill_pub`
        // pays for its ketchup half); the 48 is a mid-band mustard rate, one
        // step above `hot_dog_stand`'s 46 because this order is harder to
        // serve than either single-sauce rung. It lands just under
        // `grill_pub`'s 210 for five boxes, which is correct: BBQ is the
        // dearer case to make, so the same-sized BBQ mix must out-pay the
        // mustard one. Its blended 40.8 per box is not a rung on the mustard
        // ladder — a mixed order's per-box rate averages two sauces and says
        // nothing about commitment, per the header's qualifier.
        Contract(
            id: "deli_chain",
            name: "Deli Chain",
            mix: [("boxed_ketchup", 3), ("boxed_mustard", 2)],
            cadence_ticks: 3000,
            payout: 204,
            price: 350,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
        // The mustard pallet, and the top of this tier: eight boxes every
        // 5000 ticks is 1.6 per 1000 against the ~2.7 a 360-tick vat makes,
        // so it asks for about three fifths of one mustard line's output —
        // rather than the whole line `export_pallet` asks of ketchup. What
        // makes it the hardest mustard order on the board is not the rate
        // but the burst: eight cases have to be banked and shipped inside
        // one window, so a line running at exactly 0.58 of capacity still
        // needs somewhere to put them.
        //
        // PAYOUT: 8 boxes x 52 = 416. 52 is the top of the mustard band (17
        // in raws is 33% of it, the same fraction `export_pallet` leaves on
        // ketchup), and it is the best mustard rate on the board because it
        // is the hardest mustard order to fill.
        Contract(
            id: "sandwich_franchise",
            name: "Sandwich Franchise",
            mix: [("boxed_mustard", 8)],
            cadence_ticks: 5000,
            payout: 416,
            price: 800,
            strikes: 2,
        ),
    ],
)
